in this video we will talk about the  neuron a neuron is an electrically  excitable cell that processes and  transmits information through both the  electrical and chemical signals let's  first talk about the structure of the  neuron as you can see the majority of  the neurons are made up of three main  parts the first main part is the  dendrites of the neuron the second main  part of the neuron is the cell body and  the third wind part of the neuron is the  axon let's first talk about the  dendrites and dendrites  they usually arise from the cell body  and they are variable in number the  dendrites basically get thinner with  every branching of the nendroid  now talking about the cell body of the  neuron the cell body basically consists  of all these typical cell organelle is  like the mitochondria and a plasmic  reticulum nucleus cytosol etc one  special type of  organelle that is present in the cell  body of the neuron are these missiles  granules which are basically the  collection of endoplasmic reticulum  these are the rough endoplasmic  reticulum and the help in the protein  synthesis of the neuron now talking  about the axon of the neuron the axon is  usually a single structure which arises  from the cell body of the neuron and the  axon basically usually remains a  constant diameter throughout its length  and the point of origin of the axon from  the cell body is known as the axon  hillock the axon in majority of the  neurons is covered by a myelin sheath  which helps in electrical insulation and  proper electrical conduction of the axon  and the structure you see here are the  axon terminal of the axon and these are  basically a collection of synaptic bulbs  which are connected to the dendrites of  the further axons and these axon  terminals release chemical transmitters  most commonly the GABA and the glutamate  which helped in generating new impulses  in the neurons which are connected to  these synaptic bulbs so this was a brief  description about the structure of the  neuron let's talk about how the signal  is transmitted across a neuron so  talking about the neuron signal  transmission as you can see in this  diagram we have three neurons the neuron  member - and the neuron number three are  connected to the synaptic terminals of  the axon number one so to begin the  neuron signal transmission one important  term I would like you to mention is the  resting potential which is the  electrical potential in the neurons at  their resting state and it is minus 70  millivolts so the initiating event in  the neuron signal transmission is  usually firing of the synaptic terminals  which are connected to the dendrites of  the axon number one and when the action  potential develops inside the axon  number one it causes the depolarization  of the axon number one across its whole  length and this electrical signal  transmits across the whole length of the  axon number one and when this positive  signal reaches the synaptic terminal of  the axon one it causes the release of  neurotransmitters and these  neurotransmitters interact with the  dendrites of the axon number one - and  the axon number three and the cause  development of similar action potentials  in both the axons which are connected to  the axon number one so this is how the  electrical signal transmits across the  various axons thank you so much for  watching this video I hope you find this  video helpful don't forget to hit the  like button below and also make sure to  subscribe to our Channel also don't  forget to Like us on Facebook for latest  updates about the videos
 

